Xcode app and test-runner compilation across CI checkouts (#1346)","files":[".github/workflows/ci.yml","TESTING.md","python/tests/test_swift_cache.py","scripts/swift_cache.py"]},{"sha":"6f3780338b42a8a2d544a81589ced6febeabac24","title":"Preserve published PR descriptions during final preparation (#1347)","files":["docs/architecture/delivery.md","docs/lf.md","rust/loopflow/src/ops/land.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/pr.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/land_tests.rs"]},{"sha":"567ac07df863b524f9b1ca6698161748b60b27fd","title":"Reduce repeated migration bookkeeping (#1348)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/store/migrations.rs"]},{"sha":"4439aedd9afb6deae779f646b667ab1a1e90d43b","title":"Keep doctor CLI tests on isolated local remotes (#1349)","files":["rust/loopflow/tests/doctor_tests.rs"]},{"sha":"ca18d25cb80cbf6904f69cf1291154f02a6c3f68","title":"Measure recorded agent attempts through exact Task PR merges (#1350)","files":["TESTING.md","docs/architecture/execution.md","performance/README.md","performance/budgets.json","python/tests/test_lifecycle_scorecard.py","rust/loopflow/src/controller/task/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/activity.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/flow.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/human_session.rs","rust/loopflow/src/run_record.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/dto_fixtures.rs","scripts/lifecycle_scorecard.py","swift/Loopflow/Services/RegistryQuery.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/DTOFixtureTests.swift","tests/fixtures/dto/README.md","tests/fixtures/dto/wave_detail.json"]},{"sha":"6e7189926ede81b3edd05c8d81955c6cbe67a4e2","title":"Run native surface captures two at a time (#1351)","files":["TESTING.md","python/tests/test_surface_proof.py","scripts/prove_wave_surface_states.sh"]}],"merged_prs":[{"number":1310,"title":"Realign plans and code before publishing Task work","body":"Task review bundled reconciliation with PR publication and required reports that could drift from the work. This PR adds `realign` to update plans, code, and Wave memory directly, separates publication from reconciliation, and lets contributors open draft PRs without marking them ready.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Replace `review-slice` and `refresh-plan` with `realign`, preserving accepted requirements, unresolved evidence, and remaining work.\n- Add `refresh` (rebase → realign) to each `pursue` pass. Publish after convergence, before the human demo; make publication explicit in `build` and `slice` too. Refresh includes the existing leased branch push.\n- Revise implementation, review, triage, and learning skills to update existing artifacts and leave concise evidence instead of mandatory pass reports. PR publication now preserves independent review files.\n- Make `lf pr open` create drafts and preserve existing readiness. `lf pr publish` promotes drafts; failed promotion leaves draft copy intact and supports retry.\n- Update Flow fixtures and decision-boundary tests, and use process termination notifications for Swift observation-test cleanup.\n\n## Checks\n\nBranch memory records 27 selected builtin, export, and draft tests plus all-target Clippy passing; these were not rerun for this description. GitHub/browser behavior used local simulations. Reconciliation scenarios were walkthroughs, not independent-agent trials.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed here: on a branch with changes and no PR, run `lf pr open`. Its GitHub page should show a draft. Open it again to confirm it stays draft, then run `lf pr publish` to mark it ready.","files":[],"additions":986,"deletions":864,"changed_files":68,"merge_commit":"c512813b5b333f2ae012503b1781fcbd458be67a"},{"number":1312,"title":"Simplify repair and delivery choices and add manual S1–S5 operation","body":"Overlapping skills and Flow shortcuts made it difficult to choose how to repair a failure, advance a change, or operate Waves. This PR reduces those choices, clarifies where delivery stops, and adds a manual repository pass through delivery, coordination, capacity, adaptation and identity.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `vsm-operate`, composing independently callable `s1` through `s5` skills with shared evidence. Update `wave/operate` to consider the same questions within one Wave while preserving independent Task progress.\n- Replace the old scan/assess/mutate governance pipelines and retire overlapping delivery shortcuts. Document the distinct stopping points of `code`, `queue`, `pursue` and `feature`.\n- Add `unbreak` for restoring broken workflows and compose `incident` as `unbreak → 5whys → launch-plan`. Retain `debug` for investigating and fixing code failures.\n- Fold Task-specific instructions into reusable skills. Simplify delivery prompts to delegate mechanics and PR copy generation to existing `lf` operations.\n\nBuilds on [Retire duplicate memory skills · PR #1319](https://github.com/loopflowstudio/loopflow/pull/1319) by consolidating the remaining overlapping instructions.\n\nRetired names have no compatibility aliases; custom Flow references need updating. Captured Flows retain pinned instructions, and installed skill exports require a separate sync. Manual operation adds neither scheduling nor channel delivery.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch verification covers catalog discovery, export pruning and personal-skill preservation, Flow endpoints and review returns, assembled prompts, and documentation. Formatting and all-target Clippy passed; the focused compression checks passed 26 Rust and four Python checks. These results were not rerun for this description.\n\nThe [design and verification evidence](https://github.com/loopflowstudio/loopflow/blob/9619d803ee9765de907cc529791507e822c21b57/scratch/skill-consolidation-evidence.md) records remaining limits: the configured five-step VSM evidence handoff and incident handoff still need end-to-end proof. Live planning writes and installed adoption are not established.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not yet verified end to end: with this branch's CLI, run `lf vsm-operate` from a repository with Waves. It should run S1–S5 sequentially, carry findings forward, and report useful authorized actions or an explicit no-action result. Use `lf --wave <wave> wave/operate` for a single Wave pass.","files":[],"additions":1083,"deletions":2605,"changed_files":87,"merge_commit":"ea03788fa4a5697ebb390a62bf24f54ab4b4928f"},{"number":1316,"title":"Read and edit Task files beside the conversation","body":"Reviewing and editing Task files previously meant leaving the conversation or using the terminal. The Mac app now puts scratch notes, changed files, and an editable file view beside the Task’s retained terminals.\n\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [Read and edit task files beside the conversation · LOO-327](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-327/read-and-edit-task-files-beside-the-conversation)\n> **PR lifecycle:** Merging PR 1 completes the Task.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add a **Show Files** browser with recursive `scratch/` navigation, changed files, and a PR link. Terminals and files follow the selected Task’s checkout.\n- Compare against the recorded PR base (**Parent**) or **HEAD**, with a resolved commit pinning the file list and diff. **Diff** includes unsaved edits without writing them to disk.\n- Retain drafts, selection, and Undo while switching files or Tasks within a window. Autosave runs after two quiet seconds; disabling **Autosave Task Files** enables explicit Save with ⌘S.\n- Receive external file changes live. Disjoint local edits survive; disk changes win overlaps. Saves check revisions and retain submitted drafts, displaced files, and receipts for recovery.\n- Extend `lf task changes`, `diff`, and `file`, and add `lf task save`. File access uses recorded local placement without planning sync or starting a Run.\n\nEditing supports complete UTF-8 files up to 1 MB. Unsaved drafts do not survive window closure. Recovery storage has no automatic cleanup and disappears with the worktree; it does not guarantee exclusion of concurrent writers or power-loss durability. Extremely long lines can remain slow.\n\n## Checks\n\nAdded Rust and Swift coverage for comparisons, draft retention, external changes, save races, recovery, and shared DTO shapes. Tests and lint were not run during this PR-description review.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: open a Task with a local checkout, choose **Show Files**, and edit an existing scratch file beside its terminal. Switch to **Diff** to inspect the edit, then switch Tasks and return to verify the retained document. After autosave settles, read the file in the terminal to see the saved content.","files":[],"additions":3100,"deletions":380,"changed_files":38,"merge_commit":"4e3e55658c034f3276f67351356d834b7c06d5a5"},{"number":1317,"title":"Keep saved Session launches tied to their original installation","body":"Saved review commands could lose access to their Session after the selected installation changed, and early launch failures left little evidence after retry. This PR carries the original executable and data location into the terminal and preserves returned launch failures for diagnosis.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Include the executable, Home and database in `open_argv`; recognize retained executable/store pairs through settled installation receipts.\n- Journal Session launch failures with executable path, SHA-256, working directory, Home, database and prepared Run identity, without adding prompts or environment values to the diagnostic.\n- Preserve failure evidence after retry while leaving the review pending.\n- Clarify that `lf session open <session-id> --json` prepares the handoff; executing its returned `open_argv` opens the conversation.\n\nSession listing still reads only the selected installation’s store. Retained launches require valid receipts and unchanged artifacts. Abrupt termination of the opening process can still lose failure evidence.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch validation: 122 affected tests passed. After the final journal-root correction, all 7 Session CLI tests passed, along with formatting, all-target Clippy, architecture and diff checks. Checks were not rerun for this description.\n\nProvider coverage uses simulations. The complete installation-switch journey and real Ghostty open/resume remain unverified.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not yet demonstrated end to end: prepare a disposable review with `lf session open <session-id> --json` and save `open_argv`. Select another installation, then execute the saved argv unchanged in a fresh terminal. The original review should open against its original data and remain pending until explicitly completed.","files":[],"additions":257,"deletions":27,"changed_files":8,"merge_commit":"d9632d833c8216b00cde117656d12172eda00c2c"},{"number":1319,"title":"Retire duplicate memory skills and reconcile queue work before gate","body":"Wave memory curation had three overlapping skill entry points, and shipping\nflows could change guidance after gate. This change makes `realign` the single\nbuiltin curation skill and moves queue reconciliation before validation.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Remove `update-wave` and `record-learnings` from the builtin catalog.\n- Run queue as compress → refresh (rebase → realign) → gate.\n- Remove post-gate curation from ship and ship-demo, retaining demo review and\n  Task-completing landing where already present.\n- Align prompts, goldens, user docs, and Wave memory with realign ownership;\n  explicitly verify retirement in catalog and export tests.\n\nQueue can now encounter upstream conflicts during refresh. Existing captured\ninvocations and installed exports retain their ordinary refresh behavior;\ninstalled adoption and live curation were not exercised.\n\n## Checks\n\n- 205 affected Rust tests passed, including Flow traversal, skill exports,\n  prompt goldens, launch seeds, and Task review-boundary projection.\n- Website/docs: 78 passed, 3 skipped.\n- Rust formatting, all-target Clippy with warnings denied, architecture\n  validation, and diff whitespace checks passed.\n- Full materialized migration and platform matrices remain with CI.\n","files":[],"additions":74,"deletions":218,"changed_files":23,"merge_commit":"a2b59ed50666a433c97cb9002476e74351723759"},{"number":1320,"title":"Reduce verification and landing waits while preserving disk headroom","body":"Reduce avoidable waits when verifying and landing a diff, while keeping useful disk headroom for parallel workers.\n\nChanges:\n- Reclaim inactive build caches unchanged for 24 hours toward 64 GiB free; stop verification below 32 GiB. Preserve active/recent builds, serialize cleaners, and print cleanup results.\n- Reuse identical passing evidence and return empty plans before global resource scans. Fresh execution retains capacity checks and cleanup.\n- Reconcile GitHub immediately after a landing repair completes, while keeping repeated repairs paced and requiring fresh observations.\n- Cancel superseded PR CI runs without cancelling independent main/merge-group runs. Preserve Swift test events and logs on failure to diagnose the remaining hosted hangs.\n\nMeasured locally:\n- Identical-evidence reuse: 8.02 s → 0.12 s; empty plan: 16.09 s → effectively zero. The isolated probe used real filesystem scans and receipts with live cache deletion disabled; these timings exclude CLI startup/diff discovery.\n- The unchanged CLI landing regression: 73.67 s → 13.91 s across successful repair and blocked-repair scenarios. GitHub/provider boundaries are fixtures; hosted lead-time savings are not yet measured.\n\nChecks:\n- Focused resource/gate behavior tests, including stale-cache reclamation, active/recent retention, contention, emergency stopping, and reuse invalidation.\n- All 15 landing supervisor tests and the CLI landing regression pass.\n- Cargo formatting, Clippy across all targets with warnings denied, Ruff, and actionlint pass.\n- The local Swift suite passed 331 tests; the hosted Swift hang remains unresolved. The added artifacts provide diagnostic evidence, not a claimed root-cause fix.\n\nCleanup runs before fresh verification, not on a background timer. The 64 GiB target is best effort when safe candidates exist; it is not a host-wide build reservation.\n","files":[],"additions":408,"deletions":81,"changed_files":9,"merge_commit":"192124063fa8fad6c9345cac0f672b7c0e9904a0"},{"number":1321,"title":"Reuse the integration-built CLI in CI","body":"The Rust CI job already builds `lf` for its integration tests. Reuse that executable for the checkout-refresh proof instead of compiling the CLI a second time. Check that it is executable so a missing binary cannot silently skip the Python tests.\n\nThe redundant build took 40.36 seconds in PR #1320’s passing Rust job and 50.25 seconds in an earlier sampled job. This removes that separate build; the checkout-refresh tests still run. Hosted savings remain to be measured.\n\nChecks: nextest’s actual artifact list identifies the integration-built `debug/lf`; all 19 checkout-refresh tests passed using it without an intervening build (58.47 seconds locally). Actionlint and whitespace checks passed.\n","files":[],"additions":2,"deletions":1,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"c3e8372a3b9776f5e072e156fe8056878e94f109"},{"number":1322,"title":"Restore delivery telemetry from current Run records","body":"Scheduled telemetry crashed on `agent_turns`, a table removed by the Run-ledger migration. Feed the existing Rust RunSnapshot projection to the scorecard and remove its retired usage, Run, Epoch, and global-authority SQL readers.\n\nThe report now measures ended-Run duration and direct usage, plus recorded Task PR creation/publication/landing intervals. Missing or non-final usage stays unknown. Per-Run budgets start unset instead of inheriting per-Turn limits, and unbudgeted samples do not claim a performance pass. Task-loop trust and first progress explicitly remain unavailable because their old interval facts have no current equivalent.\n\nRead-only current-Home proof produced 263 Run-duration samples, 185 total-input samples, 217 output samples, and 32 cost samples. Only two local Task PR landing samples were available; this does not establish an implementation-to-merge baseline or cover standalone PRs.\n\nChecks: seven focused Python tests, three Rust telemetry tests, and the real telemetry-daily CLI path with the actual generator passed. The CLI fixture previously substituted a fake generator; it now exercises publication with a registered metric. Cargo formatting/Clippy across all targets, Ruff, and whitespace checks passed.\n","files":[],"additions":456,"deletions":818,"changed_files":7,"merge_commit":"aab595e6ab2ca5f3ec88972ccf9c9f11458523fe"},{"number":1323,"title":"Preserve queued standalone PR heads when resuming land","body":"Resuming `lf pr land` for a clean standalone PR that is already armed or queued now keeps the existing head, merge request, and CI. New local commits and dirty source still prepare and publish a replacement head. Explicit standalone title/body edits update only the supplied fields.\n\nThis came from a live counterexample while shipping #1320: arm produced `6a3aaf18`, then land rewrote it as `3ba86691`. Both commits have the same file tree and parent, with commit times 46 seconds apart. The replacement triggered another CI matrix whose critical Rust job took 10m21s. The Task resume path already avoided this; the standalone path always dequeued and collapsed history again.\n\nThe fix extends the existing preparation owner. It does not skip checks or shorten polling, and Task merge dispositions retain their matching rules.\n\nChecks: six focused integration tests passed, proving preserved SHA/queue/copy with a rejecting push hook, dirty and committed new source publication, Task replay, initial collapse, and local merge. Cargo formatting, Clippy across all targets with warnings denied, and whitespace checks passed. Fixed replay evidence is local with fixture GitHub state; live post-release savings remain to be measured.\n","files":[],"additions":142,"deletions":11,"changed_files":3,"merge_commit":"95de0ba8b5c950dee041e5ea533072fe8e7fee92"},{"number":1324,"title":"Reuse migration schema references during store validation","body":"Repeated store validation replays the entire embedded migration chain to construct the same expected schema. Cache that immutable reference per process, keyed by the exact ordered SQL, and keep reading and comparing each actual database on every validation.\n\nCanonical and development validation now share one builder. It caches successful schema values only, retains no SQLite connections or database-validity results, and serializes cold construction so concurrent opens do not duplicate it.\n\nLocal eight-sample measurements reduced repeated schema validation from 386 ms to 1.3 ms and subsequent fresh-store creation from 876 ms to 433 ms. Cold creation and already-fast private reopens were essentially unchanged. These are local measurements, not a claim about whole CI or time-to-merge; nextest starts a separate process per test.\n\nChecks:\n- Two new behavioral proofs reject live index drift after warming the cache and distinguish different SQL under the same migration identity.\n- All 79 affected migration/shared-store tests passed in 13.039s. The unchanged baseline's 77 tests took 17.516s on the same host with four test threads.\n- Existing development draft prefix, migration history, and shared-store ownership proofs passed.\n- Clippy (all targets, warnings denied), formatting, and diff checks passed.\n\nReview confirmed actual database schema inspection remains fresh, including constraints, indexes, triggers, and foreign keys. No persistent cache, migration rewrite, or additional dependency.","files":[],"additions":82,"deletions":22,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"b3c34563b82cd08fb429ce0fa9879b263cb46ddb"},{"number":1325,"title":"Keep watching queued PRs without redundant rebase recovery","body":"When a queued PR becomes behind its base, the landing watcher currently stops and asks for a rebase even though GitHub is checking the integrated commit. This happened on #1323: the suggested push was rejected because the branch was queued, then GitHub merged the unchanged remote head successfully.\n\nKeep the existing Auto versus Queued merge-request distinction through the watcher. A queued PR remains pending; after it leaves the queue, ordinary authorization, integration and check handling resumes. Authoritative GitHub merge or closure still wins. This avoids unnecessary recovery and potential CI restarts without adding queue mutations, polls or persisted state.\n\nChecks:\n- All 18 landing tests passed, including new queued behind/dirty/clean, dequeue, merge and closure cases. These are simulated GitHub proofs; no post-change live savings claim.\n- Clippy with all targets and warnings denied, formatting and diff checks passed.\n- Updated delivery documentation; both architecture/README synchronization checks passed.\n\nReview confirmed that a queued PR's original-head checks are not substituted for its integration result. API errors remain degraded and a successful check alone cannot finish a landing.\n","files":[],"additions":121,"deletions":17,"changed_files":3,"merge_commit":"1dce0273416b00e68bd812f97191088df949d519"},{"number":1326,"title":"Remove unrelated merge polling from candidate release tests","body":"Candidate-release tests now start with their prerequisite release PR already merged. Their shared GitHub fixture previously replayed unrelated auto-merge recovery, paying a real ten-second poll per scenario before testing signing, candidate proof, or worktree ownership.\n\nRemove that synthetic PR state machine and keep all candidate assertions and production polling unchanged. Dedicated dropped-auto-merge, integration and CI-repair fixtures remain intact. The shared fixture now rejects unnecessary PR mutation/polling calls as unexpected operations.\n\nThe same four-scenario interrupted-worktree test fell from 52.518s to 8.893s locally, excluding compilation. Four ten-second polls account for the intended saving; the rest includes timing variation. Seven affected tests previously consumed 106.640 summed seconds in one hosted run, but a new hosted comparison is still pending.\n\nChecks:\n- All seven fixture consumers passed with unchanged assertions.\n- The dedicated dropped-auto-merge test passed and still exercised its real poll (11.411s).\n- Clippy with all targets and warnings denied, formatting, and diff checks passed.\n\nTest-only diff: +3/-13 lines. Review retained candidate-before-tag ordering, signing failure/retry, exact repaired commit, interrupted materialization, and dirty/divergent/active-worktree preservation. GitHub is simulated in these tests.\n","files":[],"additions":3,"deletions":13,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"3ae954e6a06ac07889dd52ae6171f7e0e11588a4"},{"number":1327,"title":"Read GitHub checks once per observed PR head","body":"Landing, release waits, and Task refreshes now read required gates and repair details from one GitHub check set for the observed PR head. This replaces two CLI reads that each fetched all checks. Live compiled-reader measurements were 0.59–0.84 seconds versus a 2.95-second median for the previous pair. These are read-latency measurements, not whole-funnel savings.\n\nThe reader follows every page, rejects partial API failures, and discards evidence when the PR or returned commit changes. Newer reruns supersede older checks within their workflow/event identity; legacy statuses remain distinct. Existing required-gate and repair-leaf classification stays in one place. No cache or new persisted state.\n\nChecks: 130 affected tests passed; after integrating #1326, the reconciled candidate-release interruption proof passed in 6.8 seconds. Focused coverage includes pagination, moving heads, partial errors, reruns, legacy statuses, cancellation, optional failures, watched landing repair, release recovery, and Task controls during GitHub quota failures. Formatting, Clippy with warnings denied, and both documentation sync checks passed. Simulated GitHub proves the edge cases; three real configured-repository reads and three fork PR comparisons supply separate live evidence.\n","files":[],"additions":538,"deletions":153,"changed_files":9,"merge_commit":"a39ab4fd605683a4763a6c03fff672485e2e2f8e"},{"number":1328,"title":"Optimize SQLite in development and test builds","body":"Debug and test builds now compile bundled SQLite at opt-level 2. Application code keeps its existing debug profile. Schema setup and validation run in many CLI/test processes; optimizing the database engine cuts that repeated work without changing validation or adding another cache.\n\nOn the same host and source, first fresh-store creation fell from 824 to 369 ms; eight subsequent fresh stores had a median of 427 versus 193 ms. Existing private-store reopens stayed about 7 ms. All 79 migration and shared-store boundary tests passed in both runs, with execution falling from 12.99 to 5.97 seconds at four test workers. These are local measurements, not a whole-funnel or hosted saving.\n\nThe first probe build took 36.96 seconds versus 24.60 before, including the newly optimized dependency rebuild. Later suite builds took 34.66 versus 33.66 seconds. SQLite's local archive grew from 3.9 to 7.8 MiB including debug metadata. The runtime win repeats across processes; the dependency build cost is cached.\n\nOnly eight Cargo configuration lines ship. The benchmark probe was removed. Formatting and all-target Clippy with warnings denied passed; the full CI matrix supplies hosted validation.\n","files":[],"additions":8,"deletions":0,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"97e0926da089bdf2a08ec0b0d279a1248dba308e"},{"number":1329,"title":"Reuse the Swift test build for the rendering proof","body":"The Swift rendering proof now uses the LoopflowMac executable built by the preceding Swift test step. That test target already depends on the app. An executable check makes the prerequisite explicit before running the unchanged eight-capture rendering proof.\n\nOn main CI 36514192142, the test step linked LoopflowMac, then the rendering step rebuilt it with different debug flags and spent another 54.20 seconds compiling. This removes that duplicate build; it does not skip Swift tests, the Xcode compile gate, or any image assertion. The script keeps its standalone missing-binary build fallback.\n\nChecks: actionlint 1.7.12 and diff checks passed. The prior hosted log confirms the executable was produced by the test step. No local rendering pass is claimed; this PR's full hosted workflow must prove the new path, all eight distinct captures, and the observed timing.\n","files":[],"additions":2,"deletions":1,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"bc51da30a49c5db626e2677054f4d550bbbd124e"},{"number":1330,"title":"Preserve CI while armed PRs wait for merge-queue entry","body":"An armed PR can be behind main while its original CI finishes and it waits to enter GitHub’s merge queue. Landing previously demanded a rebase in that interval, discarding useful CI progress. Preserve the queue target in the existing merge-request observation and keep waiting on the same head.\n\nLanding and release now share the integration decision. Real conflicts and failed checks still receive recovery before queue entry; queued work waits for GitHub’s integrated proof. Ordinary auto-merge and revoked requests retain their handling.\n\nLive evidence: #1329 entered the queue on its unchanged head after the old watcher had requested a rebase. This patch adds no API round trip, persistent model, or polling change.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Seven focused behavioral proofs passed; affected gate: 133 tests passed.\n- Cargo formatting and all-target Clippy with warnings denied passed.\n- Two architecture documentation checks passed.\n- Production query/projection exercised against live #1329; watcher and release recovery scenarios verified with simulated GitHub responses.\n","files":[],"additions":144,"deletions":30,"changed_files":6,"merge_commit":"b42d532052f734bf2ff478f8e83c874b339ccef8"},{"number":1331,"title":"Make Wave selection proof independent of creation order","body":"Home startup selects the right Waves without promising creation order. Its test expected insertion order and intermittently failed when fixture creation crossed a clock second: main CI 36516013244 aborted after 234.989s with the correct two Waves reversed.\n\nGive the fixture distinct timestamps to reproduce that ordering deterministically, then compare the sorted exact list. Missing, extra, duplicate, invalid-policy and remotely placed selections still fail. Production behavior is unchanged; no sleeps or retries.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Original assertion reproduced the exact hosted failure; corrected assertion passed.\n- All three affected Wave host tests passed.\n- Cargo formatting, all-target Clippy with warnings denied, and diff checks passed.\n","files":[],"additions":12,"deletions":4,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"d0594bfce49deed7658b4462499a7492caf14c2e"},{"number":1332,"title":"Cache Rust dependencies with their workspace build profiles","body":"CI restores an exact Rust dependency-cache hit after workspace profile changes, recompiles SQLite, then declines to save the rebuilt artifacts. The cache action discovers member manifests and omits this repository’s virtual root Cargo.toml, where the profiles live.\n\nInclude a fingerprint of the parsed workspace profiles in all five CI Rust cache keys. Version-only releases and formatting changes keep the key; profile changes refresh it. Main publishes the shared caches while PR and merge-group runs restore without accumulating private copies. Every build and test still runs.\n\nThe current hosted logs show about 50.6s on Linux and 25.9s on macOS between SQLite compilation starting and rusqlite compilation starting, despite an exact cache hit. Those intervals include concurrent work. New keys require an initial cold build; hosted cache population followed by successful warm reuse will establish the gain.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Profile changes invalidate; version/formatting-only changes reuse; malformed TOML fails.\n- Python suite: 239 passed, 19 CLI-dependent checkout-refresh cases skipped.\n- Ruff lint/format, Actionlint 1.7.12, and diff checks passed.\n","files":[],"additions":83,"deletions":5,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"4820e3670c0e4c85a364c4f15a46978384adc7d7"},{"number":1333,"title":"Skip old event histories in recent Run queries","body":"Date-bounded Run reads parsed every event log before discarding old Runs. Landing’s repair reporter invokes that reader about every ten seconds, so parallel landing watchers repeatedly processed roughly 914 MB of unrelated local history.\n\nSkip old, valid manifests before event reduction. Selected Runs still use the existing snapshot reader, inclusive date boundary, usage reduction, gap handling and sorting. All-history queries keep their original single-read path. No snapshot cache, secondary index or storage change.\n\nThree live samples per window reduced median reader time from 7.41s to 0.18s for one hour, 7.35s to 0.66s for one day, and 7.29s to 0.15s for an empty future window. Run counts, usage-stream counts and gap counts matched. These are reader measurements on the live Home, not an overall time-to-land percentage.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Fixed fixture proves exact selected projections, inclusive boundary, ordering, usage and truncated-event evidence gaps.\n- All 85 affected Run-reader and landing tests passed.\n- Cargo formatting and all-target Clippy with warnings denied passed.\n- Temporary live benchmark probe removed; production change +11/-1 including documentation.\n","files":[],"additions":57,"deletions":1,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"fdcd4847edd07a92a1db5efce3a097665ea1ca63"},{"number":1334,"title":"Stop native proof waits when their condition succeeds","body":"The native Task Runs and Comments proofs reevaluate their wait condition thirty times even after it succeeds. Each condition can walk the full SwiftUI view tree; the loop’s where clause only suppresses the sleep.\n\nReturn on the first successful condition. Keep the same thirty-attempt budget, settling delay, native views, held-read/failure scenarios, terminal surfaces and every assertion. Production behavior is unchanged.\n\nTwo interleaved local comparisons passed the identical two proofs in 16.176s → 6.276s and 15.666s → 6.237s, excluding builds. Hosted effect remains to be measured.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Both affected native suites passed in both comparisons.\n- Final source restored after the repeat; diff checks passed.\n- Test-only change: +8/-2 lines, no retries or timeout changes.\n","files":[],"additions":8,"deletions":2,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"b315044e09c52dbe65c9f906a499ea4368035a17"},{"number":1335,"title":"Keep unchanged Cargo builds fresh when packed refs are absent","body":"Fresh checkouts may have no `.git/packed-refs`. The build script registered that missing file as a Cargo input, making every invocation rebuild Loopflow. In the E2E job, even the third call to the same unchanged CLI recompiled for 40.78 seconds.\n\nWatch packed refs only when the file exists. Keep HEAD and the loose heads/tags directories watched, so commits, tag changes and packing still refresh embedded provenance. No version, migration, builtin or E2E behavior changes.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Cargo reproduced the baseline invalidation as `stale: missing .../.git/packed-refs`.\n- Ten checks using the actual build script in a disposable Cargo fixture verified clean reuse, source edits, commits, loose and packed tags, packed-tag deletion, and detached HEAD. Unchanged executables stayed untouched. This fixture is controlled proof, not a hosted timing result.\n- Real local E2E passed; final CLI invocation reused its build in 0.09s. The source checkout already had packed refs, so hosted savings remain to be measured.\n- All-target Clippy with warnings denied, Cargo formatting and diff checks passed.\n- Production change: +5/-1 lines.\n","files":[],"additions":5,"deletions":1,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"2283951248481dd42870a1fb4cea8d96ed384e42"},{"number":1336,"title":"Reuse the merge queue’s exact CI proof on main","body":"Main repeats the full CI matrix after the merge queue has already passed the identical commit. The observed #1334 main run consumed 24.4 runner-minutes, including 9.4 Mac runner-minutes, duplicating its successful queue proof.\n\nFind the successful CI merge-group run for the exact pushed SHA and link that proof in the main summary. PRs and merge groups keep the full matrix. Missing, failed, mismatched or unreadable evidence also runs the full matrix.\n\nMain retains all five shared Rust cache jobs and the SwiftPM cache. Exact warm hits can skip repeated checks; cache misses execute each job's existing checks and populate its cache. Failed or cancelled jobs still fail aggregation, and skipped checks without proof fail. No new compile-only path, retries or weaker assertions.\n\n## Checks\n\n- 19 focused tests cover exact proof selection, missing/error cases, and the actual result-aggregation shell.\n- Live GitHub reads found #1334's exact successful queue run and rejected #1335's PR-only head.\n- Python affected suite: 258 passed, 19 CLI-dependent checkout cases skipped. Ruff check/format and Actionlint 1.7.12 passed.\n- Full PR and queue CI remain required. Main reuse and runner savings will be verified after merge; the prior runner-minute figure is a baseline, not a claimed saving.\n","files":[],"additions":239,"deletions":6,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"5402d93974dba3b6e94bea1d411951a9dfeb20d9"},{"number":1337,"title":"Filter Run ownership before parsing unrelated event histories","body":"Task, Wave, Project and parent-Run queries reduced every candidate event history before filtering by owner or parent. A scoped query could parse roughly a gigabyte of unrelated history even when it returned no Runs.\n\nSelect validated manifests first, then use the existing snapshot projection and usage reducer. WorkCatalog resolves the same recorded subjects before event parsing, including inherited ownership and renamed ancestors. Keep date boundaries, parent identity, sorting, gaps and activity's start-or-finish window. No cache, index or storage change.\n\nThree real CLI samples per query reduced median Task lookup from 7.176s to 1.269s, parent lookup from 7.486s to 0.824s, and an empty Task filter from 7.182s to 0.432s. Peak per-process RSS fell from roughly 318 MiB to 168–182 MiB. These read live Run files through a disposable Home and consistent catalog copy; they are not whole-PR or host-memory measurements. Counts, usage streams and evidence gaps matched; fixed fixtures prove exact selected snapshots.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Inclusive date/usage/gap, inherited ownership after rename, and exact uncapped parent projection proofs passed.\n- All 102 affected Rust reader, catalog, activity, usage, Work and landing tests passed.\n- Cargo formatting, all-target Clippy with warnings denied, and diff checks passed.\n- Production +57/-43; temporary measurement probes remain outside the repository.\n","files":[],"additions":161,"deletions":46,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"7c2f53b9e91564ae0ac37618098bbff065f1af9e"},{"number":1338,"title":"Keep unrelated worker commits from rebuilding the CLI","body":"A commit in another worktree currently invalidates an unchanged Loopflow CLI build because Cargo watches every local branch. Watch this checkout’s symbolic HEAD instead, retaining HEAD, tags, and packed refs. Packed branches watch their existing parent until a loose ref is created.\n\nThe real local CLI rebuilt in 23.73s after an unrelated research-branch commit. With this change, another unrelated commit left the same build fresh in 0.10s. This measures local build reuse, not overall PR lead time. Shared packed-ref and tag changes still invalidate builds.\n\nChecks: 18 Cargo-driven cases using the actual compiled build script preserved embedded versions across own commits, dirty source, branch switches, loose/packed tags, packed-to-loose refs, and detached HEAD; unrelated loose branches retained the executable. The real E2E smoke test, all-target Clippy with warnings denied, formatting, and diff checks passed.\n","files":[],"additions":18,"deletions":5,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"c509f68a848c7c4b8b20b7c366d565d00a9dd21d"},{"number":1339,"title":"Read PR merge state and queue membership together","body":"Landing could read an open PR, then read its auto-merge request after GitHub had merged it and removed the request. That combination falsely blocked a successful landing. Release could similarly try to re-arm completed work.\n\nRead PR state, head, merge commit, auto-merge and queue membership together through the existing GraphQL boundary. Landing and release share this response; the separate release view reader is removed. Truly unarmed PRs, queue waiting, exact-head repairs and cancellation retain their behavior. Cached REST Task reconciliation is unchanged.\n\nA controlled real-CLI test reproduced the missing-request failure before the change and passes with the combined observation. Three live comparisons on an open PR measured median observation latency of 1.065s for the previous two reads versus 0.676s for one. This is API latency, not full PR lead time; the original incident’s final response pair was not retained.\n\nChecks: 212 affected tests passed, covering landing/release repairs, queued and unarmed work, PR range handling, and malformed/partial responses. All-target Clippy with warnings denied, formatting and diff checks passed. Review confirmed queue cancellation retains the PR identity and no retry or polling delay masks a real missing request.\n","files":[],"additions":333,"deletions":156,"changed_files":9,"merge_commit":"7e5f32ce607f7ed744c685b2f7639b304455460c"},{"number":1340,"title":"Record CPU and memory use in CI job logs","body":"Recent Rust CI runs slowed across thousands of matching tests, but elapsed time alone could not distinguish extra CPU work, waiting, or memory pressure. Add native resource reports to the expensive Rust, Swift and Xcode commands in their existing job logs.\n\nThe reports include elapsed time, user/system CPU time, maximum RSS, faults and context switches. Existing command arguments, exit status, cache conditions, timeout and Swift pipefail behavior stay intact. TESTING.md explains platform units and that these accounting peaks are not simultaneous host/process-tree memory. Skipped proof has no fresh sample; cancellation may prevent a final report.\n\nChecks: local macOS probes preserved streamed output, success, exit code 7, and signal termination while reporting a touched 64 MiB allocation. All 19 existing CI-proof tests, Actionlint 1.7.12 and diff checks passed. The hosted matrix supplies the first Linux/macOS command measurements; instrumentation itself is not a claimed speedup.\n","files":[],"additions":25,"deletions":8,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"21c020035e20a196f71ac03433d05f996453aca7"},{"number":1341,"title":"Keep cache cleanup from stalling behind active uv workers","body":"Resource preflight could spend five minutes waiting for `uv cache prune` before running verification. The parent `uv run` itself holds a reader lock, and parallel workers can keep that cache busy indefinitely.\n\nSet `UV_LOCK_TIMEOUT=0` only for the prune child. uv retains ownership of cache locking and removal; a busy cache reports its original failure immediately, allowing other cleanup and above-reserve verification to continue. Document idle-cache maintenance and isolate two existing cleanup tests from the live host lock.\n\nObserved live: the previous invocation reported a 300-second lock timeout, then reclaimed 1.97 GiB from a stale inactive build. The fixed configured command finished in 15.9 seconds including disk scans, with the zero-second timeout preserved in its report. No uv entries were deleted while locked, and active/recent builds remain protected. Whole-command timings are not paired: the stale build was already gone in the second run.\n\nChecks: real uv locked/idle regression fails before the fix and passes after; 52 resource/gate tests pass while the host cleaner lock is deliberately held. Ruff lint, formatting, and diff checks pass. Review retained failure evidence and uv's native lock rather than adding a forced cleanup path.\n","files":[],"additions":48,"deletions":2,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"1e163d20931efc7d92b54760877a62a6308143d6"},{"number":1342,"title":"Preserve cleanup capacity when worktrees have only recent gate output","body":"With eight worktrees containing only recent verification output, resource recovery exhausted its eight-root limit without deleting anything. A stale build cache was left untouched even below the cleanup target.\n\nReturn no recovery action when a gate root has no eligible entries. Recent logs stay intact and leave cleanup capacity for stale builds; actual removals and refusals retain the same root and byte limits. Empty old directories still count as removals.\n\nA real-file fixture reproduced the failure with eight and sixteen recent gate roots. The regression now reclaims the stale build with a one-root budget while preserving every recent log; an expired gate file or empty directory still consumes that sole slot. These are temporary-file proofs with simulated disk pressure, not live host reclamation claims.\n\nChecks: 54 resource/gate tests passed; Ruff lint/format and diff checks passed. Review confirmed that paths, rather than byte totals, determine whether a removal happened. No deletion quota, retention window, or disk threshold changed.\n","files":[],"additions":54,"deletions":1,"changed_files":3,"merge_commit":"566fb3981d99b430c41358a2bad3559612999693"},{"number":1343,"title":"Reuse Swift compilation across clean CI checkouts","body":"Fresh checkout timestamps made Swift recompile unchanged sources after restoring its build cache. The cache key also depended only on Package.resolved, leaving the same week-old workspace outputs in use.\n\nKey the cache by tracked Swift inputs, compiler, and SDK. Save source hashes, modes, and timestamps with successful main builds; restore a timestamp only when the current file's content and mode still match. Changed inputs rebuild normally. PR and merge-group jobs restore shared caches; only main saves them. Every existing required test and proof-reuse condition remains in place.\n\nLocal workspace evidence: touching source timestamps caused a 23.52-second rebuild. Restoring matching timestamps avoided compilation in 0.32 seconds; recreating all 183 tracked files with new inodes still reused compilation in 1.12 seconds. A real executable fixture verified that a same-size content change recompiles and changes its output. These are local measurements, not a hosted speedup claim.\n\nThe first cache needs a cold fill. Changed Swift inputs or toolchain can require main to run its existing cache-miss verification path and save a new cache. Missing or invalid timestamp metadata keeps checkout timestamps and rebuilds normally.\n\nChecks: 267 Python tests passed, with 19 existing skips requiring a local CLI binary. Real Swift build/output probes passed; Ruff, formatting, Actionlint, and diff checks passed. Review added file-mode identity so executable-bit changes in resources cannot be hidden.\n","files":[],"additions":241,"deletions":5,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"d4b283a873804c18ef1894d49bb690d67f4d59f5"},{"number":1344,"title":"Embed the canonical schema reference at build time","body":"Fresh CLI and test processes repeatedly built the same canonical reference database before validating their real store. Generate that immutable schema during compilation so first validation can compare immediately, while still detecting changes to the actual database on every call.\n\nThe canonical registry and schema projection each have one shared source module. Release canonicalization and migration/architecture checks follow the moved registry. Drafts, historical prefixes and custom SQL retain their existing reference-construction path; shipped SQL is unchanged.\n\nLocal fresh-database tests dropped from roughly 325 ms to 180 ms across five alternating pairs. A repeated store-suite comparison improved from 11.312 s to 8.199 s, including one additional test, with no competing compiler observed. Build-script execution adds 142 ms per rebuild and embeds 32.6 KB of schema data.\n\nHosted Rust build plus tests took 277.87 s versus 338.52 s in the immediately preceding PR run. Compilation stayed near 115 s; nextest fell from 221.644 s for 2,158 tests to 160.111 s for 2,159 tests. The 2,158 matching test durations sum to 874.185 s before and 629.811 s after. These are separate hosted runners, not a controlled whole-funnel experiment. Reported peak child RSS was higher (5,276,220 versus 4,857,272 KiB); it is not aggregate runner memory.\n\nChecks: the full hosted matrix passed, as did 195 affected local Rust tests, 82 Python tests, all-target Clippy, Cargo formatting, Ruff, and the real migration/architecture checks. The added proof compares the embedded reference with complete runtime migration replay and rejects a subsequently dropped index. Review preserved backup fingerprint access to the shared schema fields without changing their representation.\n","files":[],"additions":810,"deletions":739,"changed_files":18,"merge_commit":"76c8f8af404b424c454b5dcd945027e97e28a12c"},{"number":1345,"title":"Preserve a single authored commit through final preparation","body":"A published PR with one commit was rewritten during `lf pr arm`, `submit`, or `land` even when its source and base were unchanged, starting CI again. Preserve that commit when it already has the required final history shape.\n\nThe existing integration owner still records a recovery ref, pins the fetched target, performs any necessary rebase, verifies the result, and publishes the exact head. Checkpoint and merge histories still collapse. This does not remove normalization for multi-commit PRs.\n\nThe real-Git regression failed before the fix and now proves SHA preservation through arm and submit, plus necessary rebasing when main advances. The affected landing/rebase/range suites pass 55 tests; all-target Clippy and formatting pass. Rebase fixtures also use the existing isolated Home helper so a fresh checkout does not require a developer's registry to run the tests.\n","files":[],"additions":106,"deletions":3,"changed_files":5,"merge_commit":"359c9a6c351da21e46c372afa2096f5c672f3cbd"},{"number":1346,"title":"Reuse Xcode app and test-runner compilation across CI checkouts","body":"CI currently rebuilds the Xcode app and signed test runners even when their Swift inputs are unchanged. Reuse that compilation across checkouts while retaining the fallback configuration's compile check and the current Rust control-tool build.\n\nExtend the existing Swift cache helper with a separate Xcode index and key, including Xcode, SDK and XcodeGen versions. Restore exact source keys only; changed inputs start fresh, including entitlement state. Only main writes the cache, and cache misses still compile when main can reuse merge-group proof.\n\nLocal checkout recreation triggered 202 Swift compile log entries and took 19.5s. Restoring matching timestamps eliminated recompilation; final-path repeats passed in 2.6s and 1.8s. A deliberate source error remained visible. A repeated un-restored baseline hit Xcode's entitlements timestamp error; it is excluded from timings and motivated avoiding cross-source fallback. Local storage cost is about 685 MiB, compressed to 201 MiB. Hosted warm reuse remains to be verified.\n\nChecks: 272 Python tests passed, 19 existing CLI-dependent skips; real signed Xcode app/test-runner builds passed; Ruff, formatting, Actionlint and diff checks passed. Builds on [Reuse unchanged Swift compilation · #1343](https://github.com/loopflowstudio/loopflow/pull/1343).\n","files":[],"additions":87,"deletions":22,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"7d1158dac2f0a7db420efadc76dcce20da05e2c1"},{"number":1347,"title":"Preserve published PR descriptions during final preparation","body":"Preparing an unchanged published PR currently launches an agent to rewrite its description. On #1346 this took 23 seconds and discarded recorded test results. Preserve GitHub's current title and body when the remote and local heads match, avoiding that generation step.\n\nExplicit copy and valid gate output still take precedence; new local commits still generate fresh copy. Task merge-disposition text is normalized afterward. The read happens before integration or push; ordinary publication is unchanged because its copy resolver runs after pushing. Head equality does not establish when somebody last edited the description.\n\nChecks: the new real-Git fixture first reproduced the provider dependency, then passed unchanged arm/submit, explicit body override and changed-head generation. All 51 affected delivery tests passed (one existing installation-only skip); all-target Clippy, formatting and diff checks passed. Three live GitHub reads of the original PR took 0.55/0.48/0.49 seconds and retained its exact body; this is query cost, not a whole-landing speedup claim.","files":[],"additions":158,"deletions":2,"changed_files":5,"merge_commit":"6f3780338b42a8a2d544a81589ced6febeabac24"},{"number":1348,"title":"Reduce repeated migration bookkeeping","body":"Fresh stores repeatedly attempted checksum updates for the entire migration catalog and reread the ledger before each receipt. Fill only missing checksums and hash the already validated canonical prefix directly. Migration SQL, receipt bytes, transaction boundaries and corruption checks stay unchanged.\n\nIn 32 local public `SqliteStore::new` opens per version, alternating old/new native binaries, median fresh initialization fell from 163.3 ms to 151.4 ms (7.3%). Reopens stayed near 5.9 ms. All checksum, parent-history and build-provenance fields matched the baseline; each fresh store retained its own Home identity. This is local evidence, not a claim about whole-PR latency or hosted runners.\n\nChecks: 156 affected store tests passed, including frozen historical receipt hashes, no-write current opens, rollback after a checksum mismatch, canonical upgrades and development-draft adoption. All-target Clippy, formatting and diff checks passed. Temporary benchmark code is excluded.\n\nReview: both receipt writers validate the ordered history before taking prefix slices; backfill never overwrites an existing checksum, and validation still rejects unknown or corrupt history. No new cache or alternate migration representation.\n","files":[],"additions":86,"deletions":19,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"567ac07df863b524f9b1ca6698161748b60b27fd"},{"number":1349,"title":"Keep doctor CLI tests on isolated local remotes","body":"Doctor CLI tests gave `lf` a temporary Home, but its freshness check still fetched `origin/main` into the checkout recorded in the binary. Parallel test workers therefore shared a repository mutation and depended on the network.\n\nUse the existing disposable repository and bare local remote for both doctor/telemetry fixtures. Bind child Git commands to that repository and allow only file transport. The real freshness fetch still runs; `FETCH_HEAD` proves it reached the fixture. Existing build-revision, continuity and telemetry scorecard assertions remain intact. Production doctor behavior is unchanged.\n\nThree alternating native comparison pairs reduced median doctor-test time from 1.56s to 0.58s and telemetry-test time from 3.23s to 1.03s. These are local fixture measurements, not whole-PR timing claims.\n\nChecks: all 21 affected doctor/build-info/CLI tests passed, along with all-target Clippy, formatting and diff checks.\n\nReview: rewriting only the remote URL would still modify the shared checkout. Per-process Git directory/worktree binding removes that side effect without a production seam or a canned diagnostic response.\n","files":[],"additions":11,"deletions":2,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"4439aedd9afb6deae779f646b667ab1a1e90d43b"},{"number":1350,"title":"Measure recorded agent attempts through exact Task PR merges","body":"Delivery timing currently starts at Task PR creation or publication, leaving recorded agent execution disconnected from the PR it produced. The scorecard now reports **Recorded agent attempt → merge** for exact managed Task PRs, with measured/eligible coverage.\n\nManaged Flow membership captures the PR identity, including prepared Sessions whose ownership must survive launch. The existing Run event scan projects the first provider-attempt timestamp across retries. The scorecard includes earlier and unfinished Runs for this join while preserving the existing ended-Run window for usage statistics. Rust and Swift share the updated optional fields and fixture.\n\nThis is an observed lower bound, not first-ever implementation start: missing, pruned, uninstrumented or standalone work can hide an earlier attempt. Prepared Sessions without an attempt remain unknown, and no numerical budget is invented. Task PR creation → merge remains the durable baseline.\n\n## Checks\n\n- 102 affected Rust tests, 9 scorecard Python tests, 16 Swift DTO tests passed.\n- All-target Clippy, cargo fmt, Ruff and Swift platform-boundary checks passed.\n- Synthetic generator demo: one attributed attempt and one unstarted Session report 1/2 coverage, 40 seconds, UNKNOWN; ordinary Run statistics remain unchanged.\n- Review corrected a reporting-window edge to preserve integer-second inclusion; tested prepared ownership, retry timestamps, malformed/incomplete events, exact PR joins and missing history.\n","files":[],"additions":406,"deletions":56,"changed_files":16,"merge_commit":"ca18d25cb80cbf6904f69cf1291154f02a6c3f68"},{"number":1351,"title":"Run native surface captures two at a time","body":"The native surface proof waited for eight app captures serially. It now runs the two widths of each state together, cutting local proof time from about 28 seconds to 14 seconds while producing byte-identical images.\n\nConcurrency is capped at two app processes. All eight captures, the 2.5-second render-settling delay, and every pairwise image comparison remain. The script tracks and reaps its own capture children on completion or interruption, including macOS Bash 3.2 empty-array handling.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Native serial 28.76s → paired 13.90s; reversed comparison paired 14.491s vs serial 28.234s. All eight hashes match across all four passes.\n- Sampled app RSS sum: 202,448 KiB paired vs 100,976 KiB serial; at most two apps alive. This is sampled RSS, not full-system physical memory.\n- Three behavior tests pass: bounded overlapping captures, missing-output rejection, and interruption cleanup.\n- Ruff/format, Bash syntax, and diff checks pass. Hosted CI verifies the full configured path.\n","files":[],"additions":174,"deletions":12,"changed_files":3,"merge_commit":"6e7189926ede81b3edd05c8d81955c6cbe67a4e2"}],"decisions":null,"previous_release_notes":"# v0.12.24\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.12.24 makes account access, Task execution, and unattended releases easier to inspect and recover. Authentication moves into one command family with retained usage observations, while branch builds keep private data and managed Tasks use their owning installation. Scheduled releases can hand failed checks to the landing repair supervisor and report failures back to cron.\n\n## Inspect accounts and usage in one place\n\nLogin and account inspection now share `lf auth`. Browser choices are remembered independently for managed accounts and local services, and successful usage responses remain available for offline inspection.\n\n- Use `lf auth status` or `lf auth status --details` to inspect account IDs, cached usage windows, their age and reset time, and remembered browser bindings. `lf auth status codex --verify` refreshes observations for the selected account set.\n- Native browser callback completion and explicit hidden manual input replace Chrome page and clipboard scraping. New managed accounts are staged and checked for identity before registration.\n- Status distinguishes managed credentials, local tokens, and uninspected forwarding, keeping missing credentials visible. Verification saves returned usage windows atomically while preserving routing and cooldown state.\n- Account and route listings show IDs beside logins. Headless Runs record the account selected for each attempt before a provider Session ID exists.\n\n## Keep development data separate from installed work\n\nA branch build now seeds a private, source-specific data directory once, preserving subsequent private writes and clearing inherited execution authority. Managed Task operations route to the selected installation before preparation, checkpoints, or worker claims, keeping its executable and database together.\n\n- `lf task run` starts or continues saved work; `lf task restart` replaces its invocation. Preparation is now `lf task checkout`, which can restore missing checkouts from retained history and work from dirty checkouts.\n- Managed operations return installed Task state and consume installed review readiness without transferring branch-only identities or feedback.\n- Opening a Wave chat connects it automatically. Separate Wave lifecycle and Task/Wave enablement controls are removed from the CLI, app, and wire models.\n- Task execution cannot change installations. Incompatible-database errors retain migration evidence and recommend recovery pairs only after checking their artifacts and exact database compatibility.\n\n## Recover release checks and report failures\n\nScheduled releases now use the existing landing repair supervisor when required checks fail. The release path retains version-metadata rebuilding when main advances and preserves blocked repair checkouts for recovery.\n\n- Cron receives the release operation's actual exit status. The scheduled release target resolves to the deterministic operation Flow before the same-named skill.\n- GitHub's temporary no-checks response after a push remains pending instead of being treated as a completed check result.\n- The blocking Swift fixture join reproduced locally is replaced with bounded asynchronous exit observation, addressing the cleanup defect investigated after the previous release's test hang. The original hosted job retained no stack, so that diagnosis remains limited to the local reproduction.\n\n## Operational notes\n\n- **Command migration:** six `lf auth` subcommands replace the old account/profile/access commands and top-level routing commands. Readers move to `lf catalog`, `lf wave list`, `lf wave status`, `lf wave probe`, and `lf pr checks`; removed commands have no compatibility aliases. Update scripts for these changes and the Task commands above.\n- The auth migration preserves populated account, profile, and route history. Published Rust API replacements are documented in `docs/subscriptions.md` under Rust API migration.\n- Branch and installed databases do not synchronize, so branch status can differ from state returned by a managed operation. Private databases do not isolate provider mutations or shared checkout edits. Remote placement remains supported; remote chat transport is not added to the app.\n- Recorded checks include 338 Swift tests, simulated failed-check repair before tagging, and all four disposable Linux installation proofs. Live installed-worker acceptance and live release publication remain unproven in the supplied evidence.\n- Browser login without pasted codes, first-time managed connection, remembered Linear profile targeting, and real Claude/Codex usage windows also remain unproven. The configured Claude probe returned `invalid_grant`; its decoder fixture is synthetic. A copied-Home demo retained a pre-migration detailed-status failure and a stored-route/absent-credential disagreement. Cross-account Session continuation, native credential refresh coordination, and remaining-headroom ranking are outside this release's auth scope.\n\n## Small changes\n\n- Cold installation-proof image downloads have a network deadline separate from container creation.\n- CI adds a required disposable Linux installation job covering continuation, review completion, store protection, and recovery.","source_limits":{"context_bytes":131072,"commits":256,"merged_prs":200,"files_per_change":100,"area_scopes":32,"name_bytes":256,"title_bytes":300,"path_bytes":512,"pr_body_bytes":4096,"decisions_bytes":16384,"previous_release_notes_bytes":16384,"release_notes_bytes":61440},"omissions":{"commits":0,"merged_prs":0,"commit_files":0,"pr_files":0,"area_scopes":0,"text_bytes":0,"decisions_bytes":0,"previous_release_notes_bytes":0}}